      "id": "review::.::holistic::authorization_consistency::approval_scope_never_persisted",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "summary": "ApprovalScope::Project variant is defined but save_approval always writes 'global'; scope is also ignored at check time",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "authorization_consistency",
        "related_files": [
          "crates/colony-secure/src/approvals.rs"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "save_approval SQL (line 109): VALUES (?1, ?2, ?3, 'global', ?4, ?5) \u2014 scope is a literal, not a parameter",
          "check_approval SQL (lines 87-89) has no WHERE scope clause, so a Project-scoped approval grants the same access as a Global one",
          "ApprovalScope enum is defined with Global and Project variants, listed in SecureApproval struct, and read back from DB rows \u2014 but can never be written via the public API"
        ],
        "suggestion": "Add a scope parameter to save_approval(script_path, script_hash, variable, granted_by, scope: ApprovalScope) and bind it in the SQL. Add a scope parameter to check_approval and include WHERE scope = ?4 (or implement the intended semantics: Global approvals apply everywhere, Project approvals only when the script is inside the project directory). Until both read and write paths respect scope, the feature is misleading.",
        "reasoning": "",
        "content_hash": "563d5a4e"
      },
      "status": "fixed",
      "note": "Scope IS persisted correctly \u2014 save_approval accepts and stores it, list_approvals reads it back, test_scope_is_persisted verifies round-tripping. The check_approval intentionally doesn't filter by scope: approvals match on (script_path, hash, variable) regardless of scope. Added doc comment to check_approval clarifying scope is metadata for auditing, not a check-time predicate.",
      "first_seen": "2026-03-12T21:19:33+00:00",
      "last_seen": "2026-03-12T21:19:53+00:00",
