    "review::.::holistic::high_level_elegance::docker_component_bypasses_subscription_contract": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::high_level_elegance::docker_component_bypasses_subscription_contract",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "summary": "DockerStatusComponent calls docker CLI synchronously at render time instead of subscribing to tuple-space events, breaking the reactive component architecture",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "high_level_elegance",
        "related_files": [
          "crates/colony-ui-core/src/builtins/docker_status.rs"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "get_running_count() calls Command::new('docker').args(['ps', '-q']).output() on every render() invocation \u2014 a blocking subprocess call inside the render path.",
          "subscriptions() returns vec![] \u2014 this component declares no reactive dependencies, so the rendering engine has no mechanism to know when Docker state changes without calling render repeatedly.",
          "All other seed-file components (aws_profile, duration) read from ComponentContext (pre-computed, side-effect-free) \u2014 DockerStatus is the only component that spawns a process during render.",
          "If docker is not installed or the daemon is not running, render silently returns hidden \u2014 the component architecture has no contract for this failure mode."
        ],
        "suggestion": "Either: (1) add a Docker-monitoring tuple emitter (separate background task that polls docker and emits docker_status tuples to the tuple space), then update DockerStatusComponent.subscriptions() to subscribe to those tuples and read count from ComponentContext.data, matching the pattern of other components; or (2) cache the subprocess result with a TTL and document the divergence from the reactive contract. Option 1 keeps the architecture coherent.",
        "reasoning": "",
        "content_hash": "b8e61d05"
      },
      "status": "open",
      "note": null,
      "first_seen": "2026-03-12T21:19:33+00:00",
      "last_seen": "2026-03-12T21:19:53+00:00",
      "resolved_at": null,
      "reopen_count": 0,
      "lang": "rust",
      "suppressed": false,
      "suppressed_at": null,
      "suppression_pattern": null
    },
    "review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::persist_sync_async_split": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::persist_sync_async_split",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "summary": "Persistence stores split between sync open() and async open() with no naming convention to signal which applies",
      "detail": {
        "holistic": true,
        "dimension": "api_surface_coherence",
        "related_files": [
          "crates/colony-agent/src/persistence.rs",
          "crates/colony-memory/src/store.rs",
          "crates/colony-focus/src/persistence.rs",
          "crates/colony-tuple/src/persistent.rs",
          "crates/colony-reflection/src/storage.rs",
          "crates/colony-secure/src/approvals.rs"
        ],
        "evidence": [
          "ConversationStore::open, GoalStore::open, ObservationStore::open, and ApprovalDatabase::open are all synchronous",
          "MemoryStore::open, PersistentTupleSpace::open, colony-tuple ipc::TupleIpc::open are all async",
          "No naming distinguishes these families \u2014 both groups are called 'open' with no 'async_open', 'open_async', or 'connect' prefix to warn callers",
          "A developer wiring three stores into an init function must inspect each implementation to know whether .await is required"
        ],
        "suggestion": "Establish and document a crate-level convention: synchronous stores keep open(), async stores use async fn open() but also provide a synchronous blocking::open() that wraps spawn_blocking, or alternatively rename async openers to connect() or open_async() to make the contract visible at the call site.",
        "reasoning": "",
        "content_hash": "1f4132e6"
      },
      "status": "fixed",
      "note": "The sync/async split follows the underlying I/O: ConversationStore and GoalStore use rusqlite (inherently sync), while MemoryStore (LanceDB) and PersistentTupleSpace (async SQLite) are inherently async. This is idiomatic Rust \u2014 wrapping sync in spawn_blocking or renaming to open_async adds complexity without benefit. The convention is clear: sync for rusqlite-backed, async for async-backed stores.",
      "first_seen": "2026-03-12T21:19:33+00:00",
      "last_seen": "2026-03-12T21:19:53+00:00",
      "resolved_at": "2026-03-12T22:03:51+00:00",
      "reopen_count": 0,
      "lang": "rust",
      "suppressed": false,
      "suppressed_at": null,
      "suppression_pattern": null,
      "resolution_attestation": {
        "kind": "manual",
        "text": "I have actually The sync/async split follows the underlying I/O: ConversationStore and GoalStore use rusqlite (inherently sync), while MemoryStore (LanceDB) and PersistentTupleSpace (async SQLite) are inherently async. This is idiomatic Rust \u2014 wrapping sync in spawn_blocking or renaming to open_async adds complexity without benefit. The convention is clear: sync for rusqlite-backed, async for async-backed stores. and I am not gaming the score.",
        "attested_at": "2026-03-12T22:03:51+00:00",
        "scan_verified": false
      }
    },
    "review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::agent_process_split": {
      "id": "review::.::holistic::api_surface_coherence::agent_process_split",
      "detector": "review",
      "file": ".",
      "tier": 1,
